    "title": "Sedgwick County leaders push for a law school in Wichita - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Sarah Moore ,\n4 hours ago\nWICHITA, Kan. (KSNW) — Sedgwick County commissioners are pushing for a law school to be established in Wichita.\nDuring their meeting on Wednesday, commissioners said having a law school in Wichita would address the lack of attorneys in central and western Kansas.\nSedgwick County District Attorney Marc Bennett has been advocating for a law school, commissioners said.\n“Bennett talks a lot about some of the challenges that the legal profession has in the state of Kansas,” Commissioner Ryan Baty said. “Not only are we graduating less law students, but we have less law students that are moving west of Topeka and servicing our area and western Kansas. And he sees this as a significant challenge and threat to the capacity to serve justice throughout Kansas.”\nBill incentivizing lawyers to practice in rural Kansas awaits Kelly’s approval There are only two law schools in the state: one at the University of Kansas in Lawrence and the other at Washburn University in Topeka.\nBaty said he has had discussions with both institutions. Leadership has expressed interest in opening a law school in Wichita, but nothing has been nailed down.\nCommissioner Jeff Blubaugh said this is something he wants to discuss at the next joint meeting with the Wichita City Council on June 30.\nCommissioners are also considering putting out a call to state leaders.",
    "summary": "During their meeting on Wednesday, commissioners said having a law school in Wichita would address the lack of attorneys in central and western Kansas. Sedgwick County District Attorney Marc Bennett has been advocating for a law school, commissioners said. “Bennett talks a lot about some of the challenges that the legal profession has in the state of Kansas,” Commissioner Ryan Baty said.",
